{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST supply the review prompt that each group's reviewer follows. The prompt MUST direct the reviewer not to edit files. It MUST direct the reviewer to make mechanical changes itself in the revised text it returns, and to return design questions for the user.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: Every clause in `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.review_prompt` specifies content or observable behavior of the prompt. It does not prescribe a library, algorithm, signature, type, field, or delegation through a named interface. The clauses are:\n- \"MUST supply the review prompt that each group's reviewer follows\".\n- \"MUST direct the reviewer not to edit files\".\n- \"MUST direct the reviewer to make mechanical changes itself in the revised text it returns, and to return design questions for the user\".\n\nI considered whether any clause fixes how the skill is built. The first clause says the skill supplies a prompt. It does not name a file path, format, or loading mechanism. A prompt kept in another file or location in the skill would give the same behavior and still satisfy it. The other two clauses are about what the prompt says, so they are behavioral. Whether the prompt says it is checked by reading the prompt, not by tracing structure.\n\nI also read the supplied root, `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/references/review-prompt.md`, to confirm it is the prompt. It says \"Don't edit files; make your changes in the revised text you return.\" Its \"Two kinds of finding\" section says \"Mechanical changes: make them yourself in the revised text\" and \"Design questions: return these; the user decides.\" So the root supplies the required content. No structural obligation needs further implementation evidence.\n\nbehavioral: The subject has three behavioral obligations. The prompt MUST direct the reviewer not to edit files. It MUST direct the reviewer to make mechanical changes itself in the revised text it returns. It MUST direct the reviewer to return design questions for the user. A fourth clause says the skill supplies the prompt.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_makes_mechanical_changes_and_returns_design_questions]` covers all three. It loads the shipped prompt through `review_prompt()`, which reads `habeas-requirements/references/review-prompt.md`. It collapses whitespace with `collapse`. It then calls `assert_text`, which asserts `contains` for each phrase and panics with a labelled message on a miss.\n\n- **No file edits:** the test asserts \"Don't edit files; make your changes in the revised text you return\". Removing the prohibition would fail it.\n- **Mechanical changes:** the test asserts \"**Mechanical changes:** make them yourself in the revised text, and never phrase them as questions\". This covers both the reviewer making the change itself and the revised-text destination.\n- **Design questions:** the test asserts \"**Design questions:** return these; the user decides\". This covers returning the questions to the user.\n\nThe supply clause is exercised because the test reads the prompt from the skill's own file. How the skill's workflow points each group's reviewer at that prompt belongs to the sibling requirement `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.review`. A test for it appears in the search results, and I did not reassess it here.\n\nThe requirement has no boundaries or modes beyond these. I found no gap.",
